+# Session bridges
+
+A companion extension can attach to the current interactive session without starting another renderer or replacing the terminal UI. Register a synchronous factory with `pi.registerSessionBridge()`, and feature-detect `SESSION_BRIDGE_API_VERSION` from the package namespace before using it.
+
+```ts
+import * as sdk from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";
+
+export default function (pi) {
+  if (sdk.SESSION_BRIDGE_API_VERSION !== 1 || typeof pi.registerSessionBridge !== "function") return;
+  pi.registerSessionBridge(({ session, ui, terminal }) => {
+    // Observe the existing session and terminal; returning nothing leaves ctx.ui unchanged.
+  });
+}
+```
+
+Pi calls each registered factory during interactive extension binding, before `session_start`. The context contains the existing `AgentSession`, its bound command actions, the stock `ExtensionUIContext`, and a narrow terminal handle. Factories may return a wrapped UI context; this changes what extensions receive, not which terminal renderer runs. Pi preserves the stock base context rather than feeding a previous wrapper back through a new binding. Bridges are not invoked in RPC, print, or JSON modes.
+
+The terminal handle exposes currently mounted extension components, editor text, queued compaction input, render-change subscriptions, notifications, input routing, and complete prompt submission through the stock editor path. Components are the actual live instances. Observers must not dispose them, run their factories again, or start a second terminal input loop. `getVisibleOverlayComponents()` and `dispatchInput()` on the TUI provide visibility and the same input pipeline used by the terminal.
+
+A bridge must release its own subscriptions and connections in `session_shutdown`. Reload and session replacement load a fresh extension runtime and bind the new bridge before emitting its `session_start`. Captured contexts belong to the old runtime afterward. Pi retains ownership of session changes, aborts, signals, renderer setup, and disposal.
+
+A factory failure is reported as an extension error and leaves the preceding UI context active, so a failed optional companion cannot prevent the local terminal from starting. Callback errors from render-change subscriptions are isolated from terminal rendering.
+
+The API exposes terminal component objects, not rendered snapshots or a remote protocol. The companion owns semantic descriptions, network transport, access control, and how unsupported components appear remotely. No module substitutions or load-order priority are required.
